# Project context
- Production app serving thousands of concurrent users. Prioritise correctness, security, performance and maintainability over speed of delivery.
- Stack: Next.js 16 (App Router), React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS 4, Supabase (Postgres and Auth), Vercel. PWA via Serwist. Tests with Vitest.
- Before writing new code, check for existing utilities, components and patterns in the repo and reuse them.

# Code quality
- Strict typing. No `any`, no unused code, no dead imports.
- Small, single-purpose functions and components. Descriptive names.
- Handle errors explicitly. Never swallow exceptions. Return safe, generic messages to the client and log details server-side.
- Validate all external input at the boundary (schema validation).
- Add or update tests for any logic you change. Do not change unrelated code.

# Security
- Never hardcode secrets. Use environment variables.
- Parameterised queries only. Never build SQL with string concatenation.
- Enforce authentication and authorisation on every endpoint and check resource ownership.
- Sanitise output to prevent XSS. Set secure headers and cookies.
- Rate limit public and auth endpoints.

# Database and SQL
- Every UPDATE and DELETE must have an explicit WHERE clause. Every SELECT must filter by key or condition and use LIMIT or pagination where the result set can grow.
- Select only the columns needed. No `SELECT *`.
- Add indexes for columns used in WHERE, JOIN and ORDER BY. Use composite indexes in the order of query filters. Explain the index reasoning in a comment or in your reply.
- Avoid N+1 queries. Use joins or batched queries.
- Use transactions for multi-step writes. Migrations must be reversible and safe on large tables.

# Performance
- Target fast load times: code split, lazy load below-the-fold content, optimise images (modern formats, correct sizing, lazy loading).
- Cache where appropriate (HTTP caching, server cache, memoisation) and state the invalidation strategy.
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders and large bundles. Flag any dependency that adds significant weight.

# SEO and accessibility
- Semantic HTML, one h1 per page, proper heading order.
- Unique title and meta description per page, canonical URLs, Open Graph tags, sitemap and robots.
- Server render or pre-render public pages. Target good Core Web Vitals (LCP, CLS, INP).
- Meet WCAG AA: contrast, keyboard navigation, focus states, alt text, labelled form fields.

# UI/UX
- Mobile first and responsive. Consistent spacing, type scale and colour tokens.
- Always design loading, empty, error and success states.
- Clear visual hierarchy, obvious primary actions, minimal cognitive load, fast feedback on interaction.

# Writing style
- Do not use em dashes or en dashes in any user-facing text, comments or copy. Use commas, full stops or colons instead.

# How to respond
- For non-trivial tasks, briefly state your plan first, then implement.
- Call out security, performance or scalability risks you notice, even if I did not ask.
- If a requirement is ambiguous, ask one focused question before building.
